% ============================================================================
% moment-of-force-conversions.query.adj — worked lookups over the NIST moment-of-force → newton-metre
% table.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a consumer produces to convert a torque given in a non-SI unit into SI: it `import`s the
% grounded table and asks the engine to bind the factor. No arithmetic and no factor is stated by the
% consumer; the engine returns the cell WITH the table's NIST citation as its proof, on the CPU.
%
%   ? moment_of_force_to_newton_metre(dyne_centimetre, $v)        % 0.0000001
%   ? moment_of_force_to_newton_metre(kilogram_force_metre, $v)   % 9.80665
%
% And the dimension guard: a unit of a DIFFERENT quantity has no row, so the lookup ABSTAINS rather
% than mis-converting across dimensions. The "pound" is a MASS unit (it converts to the kilogram), NOT
% a moment-of-force unit — so it is caught rather than silently mis-converted as if it were a torque:
%
%   ? moment_of_force_to_newton_metre(pound, $v)   % abstains (pound is mass → kilogram)
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative import resolves):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli moment-of-force-conversions.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "moment-of-force-conversions.adj"

? moment_of_force_to_newton_metre(dyne_centimetre, $v)        % expect 0.0000001  (exact NIST SP 811 B.9 factor)
? moment_of_force_to_newton_metre(kilogram_force_metre, $v)   % expect 9.80665    (exact NIST SP 811 B.9 factor)
? moment_of_force_to_newton_metre(pound, $v)                  % expect abstain    (mass unit, wrong dimension)
